add(71,"The allowance for a “Wide” is to be entered as such and no longer as a “Bye.”","56","80","May 19th 1828",page=77)
add(72,"A penalty for a “No ball” is introduced for the case in which no run is otherwise obtained.","70","74","July 27th 1829",page=77)
add(73,"An old provision permitting the bowler to water his run up to the wicket is deleted.","64","93","Before 1831",bold=A,page=77)
add("","The Revision of 1835: every evidence that this was the most thorough revision undertaken up to that time; it must have started in 1834 as the proposed alterations were publicly advertised before the end of that year. The Laws were presented for adoption at the Anniversary Meeting of the M.C.C. held on May 14th, 1835, when there was only time to deal with Serial Number 74, and the remainder of the code was referred to a special Committee which sat on May 19th. A Special Meeting held on the following day confirmed the recommendations of the Committee.",page=77,row_type="heading",bold=A)
add(74,"The “No ball” is altered to legalise round-arm bowling in the following terms, “The ball must be bowled. If it be thrown or jerked, or if the hand be above the shoulder in the delivery, the umpire must call ‘No ball’.”","72","80","May 14th 1835",page=77)
add(75,"Substitutes, even with consent, may not bowl, keep wicket, field at point, cover point, or long stop—in the last-named position the embargo now applies to every type of bowling.","62","94","May 20th 1835",page=77)
add(76,"A Law to prohibit umpires from betting appears later in the day than might be expected, since the selling of matches had become a scandal many years earlier. Either side is given power to dismiss an offending umpire.","46","1947","May 20th 1835",page=77)
add(77,"The limit of 38 inches for the length of the bat is introduced.","26","","May 20th 1835",page=77)
add(78,"The first “follow on” Law appears obliging a side to bat again if 100 runs behind on the first innings.","","95","May 20th 1835",page=78)
add(79,"A fresh bowler is allowed two trial balls, but if he takes even one, he must then finish the over.","57","84","May 20th 1835",sub="a",page=78)
add(79,"The umpire is again instructed to call “Over” after the four balls have been bowled.","31","","May 20th 1835",sub="b",page=78)
add(80,"The umpire is specially enjoined to call “No ball” immediately on delivery, and “Wide” as soon as the ball has passed the striker.","71; 74","89; 90","May 20th 1835",sub="a",page=78,annot="[transcriber] Two ref pairs printed: 71/89 against the first line, 74/90 against the second; by content 71->89 is the Wide chain and 74->90 the No-ball chain, so the printed line order looks crossed. Kept as printed.")
add(80,"The ball is to be regarded as “dead” on the call of “Wide,” so that no more than the penalty of one run can be reckoned.","","","May 20th 1835",sub="b",page=78)
add(81,"For an appeal to be entertained it must be made before the next ball is bowled.","44","","May 20th 1835",page=78)
add(82,"The size of the ball is specified for the first time; it must measure between 9 and 9¼ inches in circumference.","25","122","May 10th 1838",page=78)
add(83,"Although often referred to in earlier unofficial editions of the Laws, it is specifically stated for the first time officially that each wicket consists of three stumps, of equal and sufficient thickness to prevent the ball from passing through.","68","1884","May 10th 1838",bold=A,page=78)
add(84,"Trial balls are no longer allowed to a fresh bowler.","79","","May 10th 1838",bold=A,page=78)
add(85,"If one bail remains in place, the removal of the remaining bail renders the wicket “down” for the purpose of a “Run out,” without the necessity of striking a stump from the ground.","18","106","May 10th 1838",bold=A,page=78)
add(86,"Practice with the bat is prohibited during play.","","","May 9th 1839",page=78)
add(87,"The “L.B.W.” Law reverts to the 1788 version which required a ball to have been pitched straight irrespective of the line of its delivery.","67","99","May 9th 1839",page=79)
add(88,"The interval between innings reverts to 10 minutes.","29","","May 14th 1840",page=79)
add(89,"The batting side is allowed to reckon all the runs resulting from a “Wide”—the ball is no longer “dead” on the call of “Wide.” This was a decision of the M.C.C. Committee which seems to have been brought into use at once, though it was not officially confirmed until the Anniversary Meeting of the following year.","80","91","June 17th 1844",bold="This was a decision of the M.C.C. Committee which seems to have been brought into use at once, though it was not officially confirmed until the Anniversary Meeting of the following year.",page=79)
add(90,"The umpires are to call “No ball” if not absolutely satisfied that the hand is below the shoulder in delivery.","80","97","June 2nd 1845",page=79)
add(91,"A “Wide” is defined as a ball not “fairly within the reach of a batsman.” At the same time it is made clear that it is not a “Wide” if the striker moves to bring himself within reach of the ball.","89","134","June 2nd 1845",page=79)
add(92,"The umpires are required to change ends after each side has had one innings.","81","104","June 2nd 1845",bold=A,page=79)
add(93,"Subject to a request by either side being made to the umpires within one minute of the end of an innings, permission is given for the pitch to be swept and rolled between the innings.","73","98","May 3rd 1849",page=79)
add(94,"The consent of the opposite side is required to the particular player required to act as fielding substitute, and to the place in which he may field.","75","103","May 15th 1854",sub="a",page=79)
add(94,"A runner may be allowed for an injured striker, who however is liable to be run out if either he or his runner is out of their ground while the ball is in play.","","","May 15th 1854",sub="b",page=79)
add(95,"The limit of runs for a “follow on” is reduced to 60 runs in the case of a one-day match, and to 80 in the case of a game of longer duration.","78","111","May 15th 1854",page=80)
add(96,"A batsman who retires may only resume his innings by consent of the fielding side.","19","","May 15th 1854",page=80)
add(97,"The direction given in Serial Number 90 is reinforced to include not only the instant of delivery, but also the bowler's action immediately beforehand. (For particulars of the instructions issued to the umpires on this subject, vide Chapter 3.)","90","100","May 5th 1858",bold="but also the bowler's action immediately beforehand. (For particulars of the instructions issued to the umpires on this subject, vide Chapter 3.)",page=80)
add(98,"The rolling of the pitch between innings to be at the request of the side next to bat.","93","102","June 11th 1860",page=80)
add(99,"It was decided to alter the “L.B.W.” Law to render the striker liable should he stop the ball with any part of his person between wicket and wicket, irrespective of the pitch of the ball, or whether it would have hit the wicket or not. This decision was not put into practice as the M.C.C. found that the resolution was out of order owing to notice not having been given in accordance with the rules. (Vide Chapter 3.)","87","1884","April 15th 1863",bold="It was decided to alter the “L.B.W.” Law to render the striker liable should he stop the ball with any part of his person between wicket and wicket, irrespective of the pitch of the ball, or whether it would have hit the wicket or not.",page=80)
add(100,"A proposal to allow over-arm bowling was not accepted at a General Meeting of the M.C.C. in 1863, but is now approved in the terse phrase, “The ball must be bowled.”","97","1884","June 10th 1864",page=80)
